## 15. SMS INTEGRATION

Implement an SMS integration for transactional notifications.

The SMS provider must be configurable through secure backend configuration.

Do not invent an SMS provider API or pretend that SMS messages have been delivered when they have not.

### Business registration SMS

When business registration succeeds, send a confirmation SMS to the owner's registered phone number.

### Customer booking confirmation SMS

When a customer successfully submits a booking, send an SMS to the customer's registered phone number.

Example:

"Hello [Customer Name], your appointment booking with [Business Name] has been successfully submitted for [Appointment Date] at [Appointment Time]. Thank you."

The exact message must reflect the actual booking and payment status.

Do not tell customers that a booking is fully confirmed if the business or payment workflow still has an outstanding requirement.

### Appointment reminder SMS

Send an SMS reminder to the customer's phone number approximately 24 hours before the appointment.

Example:

"Reminder: Your appointment with [Business Name] is scheduled for tomorrow at [Appointment Time]. We look forward to seeing you."

The reminder must:
- Use the actual appointment date and time.
- Be sent to the correct customer.
- Use the correct business name.
- Respect the business's configured timezone.
- Avoid duplicate reminders.
- Not send reminders for cancelled appointments.

Use a scheduled backend process or reliable scheduled job.

Do not depend on a customer keeping the website open.

Maintain an SMS delivery log containing the relevant message, recipient, notification type, delivery status and timestamp.

SMS failures must be logged and handled without creating duplicate bookings.

